# A value wide enough to need more than 64 bits refuses instead of narrowing.
#
# Statement binding admits an arbitrary-precision decimal so that a wide class
# — a digest, an extension element — can be represented at all, and a codec
# built for such a class carries it (the pinned profile's digest codec frames
# 256 bits, exercised by prover-real-fill.test). The boundary is narrower than
# that: a codec that frames 64 bits, handed a wider value, keeps its low bits.
# The low 64 bits of two different values coincide for infinitely many pairs,
# so that silence would let two distinct public inputs absorb identically,
# derive identical challenges, and reach the same verdict — a binding
# collision inside the channel the seal battery exists to protect. Framing
# returns void and cannot refuse, so the interpreter asks the codec its width
# first.

# RUN: rm -rf %t && mkdir -p %t
# RUN: zkc-opt %pir-seal-full %S/../Transforms/pir-project-vector.mlir -o %t/sealed.mlir
# RUN: zkc-opt %pir-project-full %t/sealed.mlir -o %t/verifier.mlir
# RUN: %python %S/Inputs/one-vector.py %t/verifier.mlir 18446744073709551617 > %t/wide.json
# RUN: not zkc-run %t/verifier.mlir --vectors %t/wide.json 2>&1 | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=WIDE
# WIDE: [zkc-E411] value needs 65 bits; the codec for class 'rs' frames at most 64

# The same protocol with a value inside the domain passes this gate and stops
# later, at the profile's own missing supplier — so the refusal above is the
# width and not the fixture.
# RUN: %python %S/Inputs/one-vector.py %t/verifier.mlir 42 > %t/narrow.json
# RUN: not zkc-run %t/verifier.mlir --vectors %t/narrow.json 2>&1 | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=NARROW
# NARROW: [zkc-E402] sampling shape at 'query'
